# This keyboard-emulation config exists ONLY so StepMania's own "Config
# Key/Joy Mappings" screen (ScreenMapControllers.cpp) is usable. That
# screen's cursor navigation is hardcoded to `input.DeviceI.device ==
# DEVICE_KEYBOARD` -- it does not go through Keymaps.ini/GameButton mapping
# at all, by design, so it still works even if your joystick mapping is
# completely broken. With no keyboard device present at all (the earlier
# joystick-only experiment dropped this file and gptokeyb2's -c flag
# entirely), that one screen became unusable -- confirmed by the user
# ("cuando ingreso a la vista de mapeo de botones, ya no puedo controlar el
# menu").
#
# Actual gameplay/menu control is NOT wired through these keys at all --
# Keymaps.ini's [dance] section binds 1_Up/1_Down/1_Left/1_Right/1_Start/
# 1_Back etc directly to muOS-Keys' real Joy10_Bn/H-* values (muOS-Keys is
# DEVICE_JOY10 on this device, confirmed via a live gdb breakpoint -- not
# Joy1). Having gptokeyb2 also emit these keyboard events is harmless for
# normal play: since nothing in Keymaps.ini binds Key_up/Key_left/etc to
# any GameButton, InputMapper just ignores them there. It's exactly the
# earlier double-delivery-per-press bug (traced via a core dump to
# StepMania's note-judging code corrupting its own std::map iterator) that
# breaking this single binding-per-action rule caused -- so if any of these
# lines below are ever also added to Keymaps.ini, that bug comes back.
a = space
b = backspace
x = tab
y = z
l1 = q
l2 = 1
r1 = w
r2 = 2

start = enter
back = esc

up = up
down = down
left = left
right = right
